{
  "concept": "Asymptotic notation compares f(n) to a constant-scaled g(n) only for n beyond some threshold n₀: Θ sandwiches f between c₁g and c₂g, O caps it from above, and Ω floors it from below.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Three side-by-side panels sharing a common style, captioned (a), (b), (c)",
    "Each panel: vertical axis with no ticks or numbers, horizontal axis with arrow tip labeled 'n'",
    "Panel (a): upper curve labeled 'c₂g(n)', lower curve labeled 'c₁g(n)', middle wavy curve labeled 'f(n)' that dips and crosses near the left edge before settling between the two",
    "Panel (a) shaded corridor between c₁g(n) and c₂g(n) to the right of n₀ (the permitted region)",
    "Panel (b): upper curve labeled 'cg(n)', lower wavy curve labeled 'f(n)' that oscillates sharply at small n, meets cg(n) at n₀, then stays below",
    "Panel (b) shaded region below cg(n) to the right of n₀",
    "Panel (c): upper curve labeled 'f(n)', lower curve labeled 'cg(n)'; curves touch at n₀ and f(n) stays above afterward",
    "Panel (c) shaded region above cg(n) to the right of n₀",
    "Dashed vertical segment in each panel dropping from the curve-crossing region to the n axis",
    "Tick mark labeled 'n₀' at the foot of each dashed line",
    "Captions under each panel: 'f(n) = Θ(g(n))', 'f(n) = O(g(n))', 'f(n) = Ω(g(n))'",
    "Panel letters (a), (b), (c) below the captions",
    "Violation markers: small red dots/segments on f(n) wherever it leaves the permitted region to the right of n₀",
    "Legend mapping curve colors: f(n) black, scaled g(n) curves blue, permitted region light green tint, violations red"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "boundView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Bound type",
      "options": ["Θ (two-sided)", "O (upper)", "Ω (lower)", "All three"],
      "default": "All three",
      "teaches": "That the same f(n) and g(n) can satisfy an upper bound, a lower bound, or both, and that Θ is exactly the conjunction of O and Ω."
    },
    {
      "id": "thresholdN0",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Threshold n₀",
      "range": [0, 100, 1],
      "default": 30,
      "teaches": "That asymptotic bounds are allowed to fail for small n — pushing n₀ right erases early violations, and pulling it left exposes the wild behavior near the origin that the definition deliberately ignores."
    },
    {
      "id": "cUpper",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Upper constant (c or c₂)",
      "range": [0.5, 4, 0.05],
      "default": 2.0,
      "teaches": "That a constant factor can lift the ceiling until f(n) fits underneath — the bound is about growth rate, not about the size of the multiplier."
    },
    {
      "id": "cLower",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Lower constant (c or c₁)",
      "range": [0.1, 2, 0.05],
      "default": 0.7,
      "teaches": "That shrinking the constant drops the floor until f(n) clears it, showing the lower bound is equally a statement about growth rate."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The whole picture",
      "narration": "Three panels, one function f(n) compared against scaled copies of the same g(n). In every panel the dashed line at n₀ marks where the promise begins; everything to its left is ignored.",
      "state": { "boundView": "All three", "thresholdN0": 30, "cUpper": 2.0, "cLower": 0.7 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Upper bound: f(n) = O(g(n))",
      "narration": "Focus on the O panel. The green region is everything at or below cg(n). Past n₀, f(n) never leaves it — that is the entire content of big-O.",
      "state": { "boundView": "O (upper)", "thresholdN0": 30, "cUpper": 2.0, "cLower": 0.7 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrink c until the bound breaks",
      "narration": "Drag the upper constant down. The ceiling drops through f(n) and red violation marks appear to the right of n₀ — the bound now fails, but only because c is too small, not because g grows too slowly.",
      "state": { "boundView": "O (upper)", "thresholdN0": 30, "cUpper": 0.8, "cLower": 0.7 }
    },
    {
      "title": "n₀ forgives the early chaos",
      "narration": "Restore c and slide n₀ back toward the origin. f(n)'s early oscillations now stick out above the ceiling; sliding n₀ right makes them legal again — 'eventually' is doing real work in the definition.",
      "state": { "boundView": "O (upper)", "thresholdN0": 6, "cUpper": 2.0, "cLower": 0.7 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Lower bound: f(n) = Ω(g(n))",
      "narration": "Now the floor. With a small enough constant, cg(n) sits under f(n) for all n ≥ n₀ — f grows at least as fast as g.",
      "state": { "boundView": "Ω (lower)", "thresholdN0": 30, "cUpper": 2.0, "cLower": 0.7 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Θ is both at once",
      "narration": "Switch to Θ. Now both constants matter: f(n) must stay inside the corridor between c₁g(n) and c₂g(n) past n₀. Squeeze either constant and the corridor closes until violations appear.",
      "state": { "boundView": "Θ (two-sided)", "thresholdN0": 30, "cUpper": 2.0, "cLower": 0.7 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Close the corridor",
      "narration": "Push c₂ down and c₁ up. The green band narrows and f(n) pokes out on both sides — yet a wider pair of constants always exists, which is why f is still Θ(g).",
      "state": { "boundView": "Θ (two-sided)", "thresholdN0": 30, "cUpper": 1.1, "cLower": 1.0 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Asymptotic notation is a claim about a shaded region, not about individual values: pick any constants you like and ignore any finite prefix of inputs — if f(n) can be trapped in that region forever after, the bound holds.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three equal-width panels in a row on white, hand-drawn textbook style with thin black axes, no tick numbers, each horizontal axis ending in a small arrow labeled italic 'n'. Curves are smooth and monotone-ish increasing overall. In every panel f(n) is drawn in black and starts with a pronounced wiggle (a dip then a hump) in the leftmost fifth before straightening into a gentle rise; the scaled g(n) curves are smooth blue S-shaped rises. Panel (a): two blue curves, the upper labeled c₂g(n) near its right end, the lower labeled c₁g(n), with black f(n) weaving between and labeled between them; tint the corridor between the blue curves light green only to the right of the dashed line. Panel (b): blue cg(n) rises above black f(n) after they meet, labels at the right ends; tint everything below cg(n) light green to the right of n₀. Panel (c): black f(n) on top, blue cg(n) below, labels at right ends; tint above cg(n) to the right of n₀. Each panel has a thin dashed vertical from the curve meeting point down to a small tick on the n axis labeled italic n₀. Italic captions centered under each plot: f(n) = Θ(g(n)), f(n) = O(g(n)), f(n) = Ω(g(n)), with (a), (b), (c) below them. Any portion of f(n) lying outside the green region and right of n₀ should be overdrawn in red with small red dots at the violating samples. Dim non-active panels to ~35% opacity when the toggle selects a single bound type."
}

